    "explanation": "Hyponatremia\n• The putative mechanism: The hypotonicity leads to shift of fluid into the cell\n• Common causes: water intoxication, salt wasting, and Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH)\nHyponatremia Symptoms\n• Risk of CNS manifestation is related to the rapidity of serum sodium decline\n• Serum sodium <125 mEq/L, seizure threshold lowered\n• Serum sodium <115 mEq/L, seizures typical\n• Cerebral edema and encephalopathy may occur\n• Symptomatic hyponatremia is a medical emergency and is best treated with hypertonic saline (3% NaCl)\n◦ Goal is to increase serum sodium to 125 mEq/L\n◦ Typically ~5-6 mL/kg over 10-20 minutes\n◦ Avoid overcorrection",
