    "title": "A 6-year-old girl has a 3-week history of headaches, vomiting, and difficulty walking. The image obtained today is shown. Classifi",
    "scenario": "A 6-year-old girl has a 3-week history of headaches, vomiting, and difficulty walking. The image obtained today is shown. Classification • Posterior fossa tumors most common o Medulloblastoma, ependymoma, cerebellar astrocytoma, brainstem glioma (pons – Cushing’s triad) o Typically present w/ nausea, vomiting, and headache, but not seizures • Supratentorial tumors o Astrocytoma, primitive neuroectodermal tumor, atypical teratoid-rhabdoid tumor, germ cell tumors, craniopharyngioma, and meningioma o Typically present w/ headache, seizures Clinical Presentation • Often due to increased ICP (occurs in 40%) o Headache, nausea, vomiting (morning), lethargy o Seizures • Hydrocephalus: as seen in the image • Cranial nerve palsies (brainstem compression) o Paralysis of upward gaze, nystagmus, ataxia, gait disturbances Hydrocephalus Of the following, the most likely diagnosis is:",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Acutecerebellarataxia",
      "B": "Cerebrovascularanomaly",
      "C": "Astrocytoma",
      "D": "Cerebellar stroke",
      "E": "Lower motor neuron disease"
    },
    "correctAnswer": null,
    "correctAnswerText": null,
    "explanation": "Epidemiology\n• Malignant brain tumors are the most common solid brain tumors in children\n• More favorable outcome in adolescents, worse in children less than 3 years old\n• Most childhood CNS tumors are infratentorial\n• May cause hydrocephalus\n• 5-year survival improving (35%-65%)",
