    "correctAnswer": null,
    "correctAnswerText": null,
    "explanation": "• Potentially serious complication\n◦ Uncontrolled diuresis of dilute urine\n◦ Leads to volume depletion and severe hypernatremia\n◦ Uncontrollable thirst in patients who are awake\n◦ Mental status changes, seizures and brain edema secondary can occur, usually secondary to delayed recognition\n• Typically transient, <1 week\n• Defect in the synthesis and secretion of vasopressin by the neurohypophyseal system\n• Rare triphasic response:\nDI → SIADH → DI or normal Postoperative\nDiabetes Insipidus (DI) Treatment\n• Free water replacement\n◦ Oral fluid replacement inconsistent and unreliable in patients who are not awake and in young children\n• Closely monitor serum Na+\n• Vasopressin\n◦ IV vasopressin infusion has been shown to avoid fluid and electrolyte imbalance compared to other routes\n◦ Often coupled with careful fluid balance, including limited and carefully monitored urine output replacement\n◦ Notably, nephrogenic DI will not respond to IV vasopressin",
