    "explanation": "Clinical Trials\n• Phase I: Assessing safety and pharmacokinetics with testing on healthy subject\n• Phase II: Assessing efficacy with testing on small group with disease\n• Phase III: Testing on larger groups involving randomized multi-center trials\n• Phase IV: Post-marketing surveillance trial\nSample Size\n• Affects the power of a study\n• May limit the ability to detect adverse events\n• Considerations for an adequate sample size for a controlled trial\n◦ Clinically meaningful difference\n◦ Variability in measurement\n◦ Drop-out rate\nExternal Validity\n• Are your results generalizable\n• Non-representative samples can bias results\n• Data source affects study results\n◦ Diaries\n◦ Billing data\n◦ Discharge diagnostic code\nModule: Ethics in Research\nConflict of Interest\n• Evaluate whether an investigator has a conflict of interest during the course of a study\n• Results unduly influenced by a secondary interest\n• Ways to manage conflicts of interest\n◦ Evaluating study design (blinding,\nIntended or unintended bias in the\nmethodology)\n• What constitutes a conflict of commitment (competing demands on researcher’s time and loyalties)",
