    "explanation": "Vasodilators\n• B- type or brain natriuretic peptide\n◦ Produced by ventricular myocardium\n• Synthetic form is nesiritide\n◦ Use: acute cardiac failure\n◦ Mechanism\n• Counterregulatory to renin- angiotensin-aldosterone system\n• Stimulates cGMP, causing smooth muscle relaxation\n◦ Serious side effect: ↓↓ BP, renal failure\n• Other vasodilators\n◦ Dobutamine, milrinone\nNotes:\nVasopressors\n•\nVasopressin\n•\nOthers: epinephrine, norephinephrine, phenylephrine\no o\no o\nStored in posterior pituitary\n• Releasedin↑osmolality,↓BPor stress\nMechanism by receptors\n• Vascular smooth muscle (V1a) ↓ vasoconstriction\n• Pituitary gland (V1b) ↓ ACTH release\n• Renal collecting duct (V2) ↓ mediates water reabsorption Additional Mechanism ↑ Vascular sensitivity to norephinephrine Neutral impact on CO\n• Balancebetween↑SVRand↑ reflex vagal tone\nDistributive & Cardiogenic Shock\n• Distributive shock\n◦ ↓ PVR, ↓ SVR, ↑ CO, usually no pump failure\n◦ Anaphylaxis, angioedema, neurogenic\n◦ Norepinephrine, epinephrine, phenylephrine\n• Cardiogenic shock\n◦ ↑ SVR, ↓ CO, +impaired contractility\n◦ Myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, congenital heart disease\n◦ Dobutamine, epinephrine, milrinone\nSeptic & Refractory Septic Shock\n• Septic shock\n◦ Warm shock (20% pediatric)\n• ↓SVR,↑CO\n• Dobutamine + norepinephrine/epinephrine\n◦ Cold shock, compensated (60%)\n• ↑SVR,↓CO\n• Milrinone + epinephrine\n◦ Cold shock, decompensated (20%)\n• ↓SVR,↓CO\n• Refractory septic shock\n◦ Vasopressin to restore catecholamine response",
