    "explanation": "Sodium Channel Blockers\n• Group 1a – prolong action potential\n◦ Ex: procainamide, quinidine, disopyramide\n◦ Uses: atrial and ventricular arrhythmias\n◦ Toxicity: ↓ BP, autoimmune reactions, antimuscarinic\n• Group 1b – shorten action potential\n◦ Ex: lidocaine, mexiletine. Use: ventricular arrhythmia\n◦ Toxicity: CNS toxicity/convulsions, CV depression\n• Group 1c – no effect on action potential\n◦ Ex: flecainide. Use: refractory VT & intractable SVT\n◦ Toxicity: most proarrhythmic, CNS toxicity\nClass 2 & Class 3\n• Class 2: beta blockers\n◦ Prototype: propanolol, esmolol\n◦ Use: acute arrhythmia, prophylaxis after MI\n• Class 3: potassium channel blockers\n◦ Prolongation of action potential\n◦ Prototype: dofetilide, ibutilide, amiodarone\n◦ Use: atrial flutter or fibrillation\n◦ Toxicity: Torsade de pointes, amiodarone can cause thyroid dysfunction, pulmonary fibrosis",
