    "correctAnswer": null,
    "correctAnswerText": null,
    "explanation": "Potassium\n• K+ is filtered, reabsorbed and secreted by the nephron\n• K+ balance is achieved when urinary excretion exactly equals intake of K+ in the diet\n• Renal failure – impaired renal potassium excretion. Urine K\n◦ >30 mEq/L transcellular shift\n◦ <30 mEq/L impaired renal excr.\n• Hyperkalemia\n◦ Acidosis (each 0.1 unit decline in pH could increase K+ by 0.5 mEq/L)\n◦ Myonecrosis\n◦ Drugs (digitalis, K-sparing, ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, NSAIDs)\n◦ Adrenal insufficiency\nNotes:\nPotassium Regulation in DT and CD\n1. TheactivityandamountofNa+,K+- ATPase\n2. Thedrivingforce(electrochemical gradient) for movement of K+ across the apical membrane\n3. Thepermeabilityoftheapical membrane to K+",
