    "title": "The following are true with respect to spinal cord injury EXCEPT: Source: ©1998 by American Physiological Society. Gao E et al. Am",
    "scenario": "The following are true with respect to spinal cord injury EXCEPT: Source: ©1998 by American Physiological Society. Gao E et al. Am J Physiol Heart Circ Physiol 1998;274:H1023-H1031. Cerebral Perfusion Pressure - Treatment • Hyperventilation (mild, i.e. PaCO2 35-40) o Decreases intracranial blood volume by inducing vasoconstriction o Excessive hyperventilation (PaCO2 <26) may cause regional ischemia and secondary injury • Osmotherapy (mannitol, furosemide, acetazolamide, hypertonic saline) o Reduces brain volume by “dehydrating” cells – moving intracellular water to extracellular compartment o Acetazolamide and furosemide both inhibit CSF production directly o Hypertonic saline may have anti- inflammatory properties • Ventricular drain o Decreases intracranial volume by allowing direct removal of CSF • Decompressive craniectomy o Improves cerebral perfusion by increasing compliance of the cranium and by surgically decreasing brain size Notes:",
